        kind: 'parable',
        tone: 'light',
        title: 'The Extension Cord',
        body:
          'Every Sunday after service, Deacon Harold Pinder ran the fellowship hall coffee urn off one long orange extension cord, plugged into the wall behind the piano. Every Sunday, somebody unplugged it to vacuum. And every Sunday, at about a quarter past one, somebody else stood at the urn pressing the little red switch, saying, It’s broken again.\n\nThis Sunday it was Keisha, fifteen, who had been sent to fill the carafes for the seniors. She pressed the switch four times. She jiggled the spigot. She turned the urn around to look for a reset button.\n\nDeacon Pinder walked over slowly, the way men walk who have seen this before. He did not touch the urn. He picked up the orange cord, followed it with his hand across the floor, behind the piano, all the way to the wall, and held up the plug that was lying loose on the carpet.\n\nBaby, he said, there’s nothing wrong with the urn. It’s a good urn. It can make coffee for two hundred people. But it can’t make one cup by itself.\n\nHe pushed the plug into the socket. The red light came on.\n\nKeisha laughed. That’s it?\n\nThat’s it, he said. Pastor preached it this morning and you heard it. Branch can’t bear fruit of itself. The urn has all that potential inside it, but the power was never in the urn. It was in the wall.\n\nShe stood there while it started to rumble. Then she said, half to herself, I been pressing a lot of buttons lately. Grades, my mom, my friends. Trying to make myself work.\n\nDeacon Pinder nodded toward the cord. Check the plug first, he said. Then press buttons.\n\nAt 1:30 the seniors had their coffee. Keisha taped a small card above the outlet in her careful handwriting: Before you say it’s broken, check what it’s attached to. Nobody has unplugged it since. Well, almost nobody.',
