        title: 'The New Kitchen',
        body:
          'For eighteen years the Knowles family rented a two-bedroom apartment where the kitchen was so small that only one person could stand in it at a time. Mama Denise learned to cook for six in that kitchen with the door propped open and the dish rack balanced on top of the refrigerator. When the church’s housing ministry helped them buy a house on the other side of town, the kitchen had an island, a pantry, and a window over the sink that looked out on a lemon tree.\n\nThree months after they moved in, her daughter Simone came home from college for spring break and found her mother cooking dinner in one corner of the new kitchen, the dish rack balanced on top of the refrigerator.\n\nMama, Simone said. You have a whole island.\n\nI know, Denise said. I just keep ending up over here.\n\nSimone laughed, and then she stopped laughing, because she saw it was true of more than the kitchen. Her mother still bought groceries every day instead of once a week, because for eighteen years there had been nowhere to store them. She still kept the good dishes in boxes. She still lowered her voice on the phone as if the neighbors could hear through the wall.\n\nThat Sunday, Pastor preached on Israel in the wilderness. He said, The Lord got them out of Egypt in one night. It took Him forty years to get Egypt out of them.\n\nOn the drive home Denise was quiet. Then she said, I think I’m still living in the apartment.\n\nSimone spent the rest of spring break helping her mother move in for real. They unboxed the good dishes. They moved the dish rack to the counter by the window. They made one big grocery list. Denise stood at the island to chop the onions, and when she looked up she could see the lemon tree.\n\nIt’s a lot of room, she said, a little nervous.\n\nIt’s your room, Mama, Simone said. The Lord gave it to you. You’re allowed to stand in the middle of it.',
