        title: 'The Stake in the Yard',
        body:
          'When Brother Lionel Gibson’s father died, Lionel inherited the family land: four acres outside town that his great-grandfather had bought in 1921 with money saved from sharecropping. Everyone in the family knew the story of how hard it had been to buy, and how hard it had been to keep. Twice the county had tried to take it for back taxes. Twice the church had taken up a collection to save it.\n\nNow it was Lionel’s, free and clear. And for six years he did nothing with it.\n\nHe told himself he was being careful. Truthfully, he was afraid. Afraid the bank would cheat him if he borrowed to build. Afraid the county would find a reason to take it. Afraid his own cousins would want a piece if it ever made money. His grandfather had told him a hundred times: Don’t trust them, don’t trust anybody, keep your head down, hold on to what you got. So he held on. The weeds grew to his waist.\n\nHis daughter Nia, who managed a credit union in the city, finally sat him down with a folder: a plan to lease two acres to a farmer, put a small produce stand on the road, and build a house for her own family on the back acre.\n\nLionel listened. Then he said, The bank will take it.\n\nNot this bank, Daddy. I work there. I read every page.\n\nThey always find a way.\n\nNia closed the folder. Daddy, she said gently, the people who tried to take this land are dead. Great-grandaddy won. He bought it so we could be free on it. But you’re still standing next to the stake like that leash is on you.\n\nLionel went out to the land alone that evening and walked the fence line until dark. He thought about his grandfather, who had good reason to be afraid, and about himself, who had inherited the fear along with the deed.\n\nThe produce stand opened the following June. The first sign Nia painted for it read: Gibson Family Land, since 1921. Still ours. Finally free.',
